WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.800
Detection and tooling help teams find memory defects earlier, but they do not replace clear design.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:15.060
Compiler warnings, static analysis, runtime sanitizers, assertions, boundary-focused tests, and code review all catch different kinds of mistakes.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.080
The goal is to create overlapping support for the memory contracts the code is supposed to follow.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:32.860
Compiler warnings can identify suspicious code patterns: signed and unsigned mismatches, truncation, ignored results, questionable casts, uninitialized values, and possible array bounds concerns.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:39.160
Warning changes near memory access, allocation, copying, or cleanup deserve review rather than quick suppression.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:49.380
Static analysis can identify risky flows, unchecked values, inconsistent assumptions, escaping pointers, lost ownership, and paths where cleanup may be incomplete.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.440
Static tools are especially useful when pointer and size values move through several functions before they are used.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.020
Runtime sanitizers can detect memory errors during testing.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:07.720
They can expose out-of-bounds access, use after release, use after scope, leaks, and other issues depending on tool support and configuration.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:15.460
They are most useful when tests exercise boundary values, error paths, resize behavior, and unusual inputs.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.400
Assertions can document invariants during development.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:27.980
They can state that count is no greater than capacity, a pointer is non-null when count is nonzero, an index is in range, or ownership has moved to a cleanup path.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.240
Assertions are not a replacement for validating external input, but they make internal expectations visible.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.580
Fuzzing can support robustness testing at a high level by exercising unexpected sizes and shapes.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:48.800
Boundary tests should cover zero sizes, maximum sizes, empty strings, missing terminators, allocation failure paths, and cleanup after partial initialization.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.740
Tools support engineering discipline, but review and design discipline remain necessary.
